= Solution
{parent=a}

For a plane sound wave, <acoustic impedance> is $Z=p/u=\rho c$, with <pressure> <amplitude> $p$ and particle-velocity <amplitude> $u$. Continuity of <pressure> and normal velocity at normal incidence on a lossless interface gives
$$
\boxed{r_p=\frac{Z_2-Z_1}{Z_2+Z_1},\quad
\mathcal R=r_p^2,\quad
\mathcal T=\frac{4Z_1Z_2}{(Z_1+Z_2)^2}}.
$$
Matching $Z_1=Z_2$ eliminates reflection. A quarter-wave intermediate layer with $Z_m=\sqrt{Z_1Z_2}$ transforms the load to the source impedance at its design <frequency>; its bandwidth is limited. For strings, the analogous <mechanical impedance> is transverse force divided by velocity, $Z=Tv^{-1}=\sqrt{T\mu}$; continuity of displacement and transverse force gives the corresponding matching condition. These <amplitudes> use different force/velocity conventions from acoustic <pressure>, so reflection signs must be stated consistently.

= b
{parent=D12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Reflections from the two film surfaces interfere with propagation phase $4\pi nd\cos\theta_t/\lambda_0$ plus the relative reflection phase. Reflection from a lower to a higher <refractive index> contributes a $\pi$ phase reversal. For a higher-index film surrounded by air there is one relative reversal, hence
$$
\boxed{\text{reflection maxima: }2nd\cos\theta_t=(m+\tfrac12)\lambda_0,\quad
\text{minima: }2nd\cos\theta_t=m\lambda_0}.
$$
Thickness or angle variation therefore produces fringes or colours under broadband illumination. An <antireflection coating> between indices $n_0,n_s$ instead uses, at <normal incidence>, $n_f=\sqrt{n_0n_s}$ and $n_fd=\lambda_0/4$: the two reflected <amplitudes> then cancel at the design <wavelength>. Absorption, <polarization>, unequal <amplitudes> and multiple reflections determine the full contrast.

= c
{parent=D12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <Michelson interferometer> divides light at a <beam splitter>, sends it to two mirrors, and recombines the returned beams. A compensating plate can equalize <glass> traversal. For approximately axial rays the <optical path difference> is $\Delta=2(n_1L_1-n_2L_2)$, plus fixed instrument phase, and
$$
I=I_1+I_2+2\sqrt{I_1I_2}\cos(2\pi\Delta/\lambda_0+\phi_0)
$$
for coherent, mode-matched fields. Moving one mirror by $\delta L$ in air changes the round-trip path by $2\delta L$, so **one fringe corresponds to $\lambda_0/2$ mirror motion**. It measures displacement, <wavelength> and refractive-index changes; scanning the delay enables Fourier-transform spectroscopy. Mirror alignment, <polarization> overlap, vibration and source coherence control visibility. A finite coherence length limits useful arm mismatch.

= D13
{parent=Section D}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=D13}

Donor doping supplies conduction <electrons> to <n-type semiconductor>; acceptor doping supplies <holes> to <p-type semiconductor>. In the nondegenerate, fully ionized limit, majority densities are approximately $n_n=N_D$ and $p_p=N_A$, while minority densities follow $np=n_i^2$. On contact, majority carriers diffuse across the junction, exposing positive donor ions on the n side and negative acceptor ions on the p side. The depletion-region <electric field> points from n to p and opposes further <diffusion>. At <thermal equilibrium> the <Fermi level> is uniform and drift balances <diffusion>, with no net current.

For an abrupt junction with uniform doping and permittivity $\varepsilon_s$, charge neutrality gives $N_Dx_n=N_Ax_p$. <Poisson's equation> then gives
$$
V_{\rm bi}=\frac{k_BT}{q}\log\frac{N_AN_D}{n_i^2},\qquad
\boxed{W=x_n+x_p=
\sqrt{\frac{2\varepsilon_s}{q}\left(\frac1{N_A}+\frac1{N_D}\right)(V_{\rm bi}-V)}}.
$$
Positive applied $V$ means <forward bias>: it lowers the barrier and narrows the <depletion region>. Most depletion width lies in the more lightly doped side. The formula assumes the <depletion approximation> and fails once its operating assumptions break down.

<forward bias> increases minority densities at the depletion edges by the factor $e^{qV/k_BT}$. In the neutral material, the steady <diffusion>–<recombination> equation gives exponentially decaying excess minorities, with lengths $L_n=\sqrt{D_n\tau_n}$ and $L_p=\sqrt{D_p\tau_p}$. Taking the carrier gradients at the junction yields
$$
\boxed{I=I_S\left(e^{qV/k_BT}-1\right),\qquad
I_S=qA_jn_i^2\left(\frac{D_n}{L_nN_A}+\frac{D_p}{L_pN_D}\right)}.
$$
This ideal <diode> law assumes low injection, nondegenerate statistics, negligible depletion-region <recombination> and series resistance, and sufficiently long neutral regions. Its exponential comes from the bias-dependent injection barrier; there is no universal abrupt “turn-on” voltage. <reverse bias> suppresses injection, giving an approximate $-I_S$ current until additional leakage or breakdown matters.<The Theory of p-n Junctions in Semiconductors and p-n Junction Transistors by William Shockley>[Bell System Technical Journal 28, pp. 435–489, minority-carrier injection and rectifying-junction theory]{ref}

Rectification uses the asymmetric <current-voltage characteristic>; controlled reverse breakdown enables voltage references, with current limiting. Bias-dependent depletion capacitance enables varactors. <photodiodes> and <solar cells> collect optically generated carriers through the junction field and diffusion toward it; efficient <light-emitting diodes> use radiative <electron>–<hole> <recombination>, preferably in a direct-gap material. Series resistance, high injection, trap-assisted <recombination>, heating and finite carrier storage explain departures from the ideal steady-state law.
